  "headline": "Intake mapped the ticket-service request, existing contracts, conflicting assumptions and discovery questions.",
  "outcome": "done",
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "K1.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Help desk agents use the HTTP transition and requested bulk-close entry points; API consumers use GET /tickets; requesters receive notifications through the worker and mail relay; operators inspect audit and outbox endpoints. X-Actor supplies an unauthenticated actor label, defaulting to system."
    },
    {
      "id": "K1.2",
      "basis": "S7",
      "answer": "The full test suite is green and a running server demonstrates close notifications, suppression, batching, combined listing filters, audit attribution, repeat-close behavior and a 500-ticket bulk close."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Scope is the help desk service in this folder: closure notifications, duplicate suppression and batching, a closed-ticket window filter, filter intersection, bulk closing, audit attribution and their verification and documentation. Out of scope are OldTracker migration, outbox retry changes and unrelated features. This intake only reads files; it implements nothing and starts no server."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Preserve the lifecycle and final closed state, existing API and error conventions, audit append-only behavior, outbox retry and at-least-once delivery, worker-off behavior, existing data-file compatibility, cursor pagination and CLI raw column positions, except where the requested behavior explicitly changes the listing."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Existing interfaces include POST /tickets/:id/transition with {\"to\":\"closed\"}; GET /tickets with status, priority, limit and cursor returning {items,next}; GET /tickets/:id/audit; GET /admin/outbox; X-Actor; and notifier JSON lines with at, id, key, type, to, ticket, subject and body. No bulk endpoint or date-window parameter exists. Default proposal, subject to discovery: POST /tickets/bulk-close with {\"ids\":[...]} and GET /tickets?status=closed&closedSince=<UTC ISO timestamp>; discovery must settle their exact contracts, batch message shape and window semantics before implementation."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Existing transition returns 200, invalid input 400 invalid, missing ticket or route 404 not_found, invalid lifecycle transition 409 conflict and unexpected fault 500 internal, in the documented error envelope. Listing returns 200 or 400 for invalid known filters, limit or cursor. Default for the new bulk endpoint is 200 on complete success and existing error codes for rejected requests; discovery must settle its mixed-result and error response shapes."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Close only resolved tickets; closed is final. A repeat close returns 409 without further state, audit or notification changes. Pending resolution notices or resolution notices sent within five minutes suppress a close notice for the same ticket and requester; otherwise group eligible closes by requester within one bulk call and make them due on the next worker tick. For the requested weekly window, default to a rolling seven days based on closure time, inclusive at the lower bound and no later than the request time. These durations and grouping choices are assumptions, not established business rules; discovery must first examine available evidence."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Existing invalid inputs include malformed or non-object JSON, unknown body fields, invalid statuses or priorities, limits outside integer 1–100, invalid cursors, oversized bodies and X-Actor over 64 characters. Default for bulk input is a nonempty array of unique ticket IDs, with at least 500 accepted; discovery must settle duplicate IDs, missing IDs, mixed states and date validation before their contract is fixed."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The single service process owns tickets, append-only audit and outbox state in memory; DATA_FILE optionally persists a versioned JSON snapshot across restarts. Notification output is in memory or appended to NOTIFY_FILE. Audit and sent/dead outbox records are retained; without configured files, process state and in-memory notifications end on restart. Any new suppression or batching state needs an explicit lifetime and persistence decision."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The API and admin outbox are described as internal-network services, with no application authentication or authorization shown; X-Actor is caller supplied and is not proof of identity. One process owns the data file; CLI and report read it. Delivery is at least once across a crash, and the relay deduplicates by key and id. Cross-process writes and multi-device coordination are not provided; default to existing single-owner behavior and do not promise confidentiality between internal callers."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Node.js 22 or later, CommonJS, strict mode and Node standard library."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.2",
      "basis": "S11",
      "answer": "No third-party dependencies; use the Node standard library."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.3",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Existing settings are PORT=4180, optional DATA_FILE and NOTIFY_FILE, WORKER_INTERVAL_MS=500 with 0 disabling the worker, MAX_BODY_BYTES=65536 and LOG_REQUESTS off. No new configuration is established; discovery must assess whether the proposed 500-ID request fits the existing body limit and whether any new setting is necessary."
    },
    {
      "id": "K7.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Use the existing HTTP and validation boundary, ticket query and lifecycle modules, TicketStore with injected clock and persistence, audit and outbox, worker, notifier and templates. Changes to ticket state must pass through the store; scheduled work belongs to the worker."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The framework is node:test and the suite command is npm test, which runs node --test."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.2",
      "basis": "S7",
      "answer": "Exercise the actual HTTP server for single and 500-ticket bulk close, repeat and invalid closes, actor audit, and intersecting closed-window filters across pages; observe actual notifier output and outbox state for prompt delivery, suppression and per-requester grouping; verify restart compatibility and unchanged retry behavior with appropriate controlled tests. This intake has not run these checks."
    },
    {
      "id": "K9.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Deliver the eventual change in this service's source checkout and make it usable through its running HTTP service and requester notification path. The named repository is the delivery target; no separate deployment is authorized or evidenced. A later implementation run needs write access and a local server for verification. An isolated run's source checkout stays at its snapshot until final return; a deployed outcome exists only after release."
    },
    {
      "id": "K10.1",
      "basis": "S13",
      "answer": "A single bulk call must successfully close 500 eligible tickets; no numerical latency target is specified. Preserve the standard-library-only constraint and existing internal-network security model. No accessibility or visual interface requirement is stated. Prompt delivery is bounded by the existing worker interval and cannot be guaranteed while the worker is disabled or delivery fails."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Likely rejection risks are an invented weekly window, a suppression rule that hides needed notices, unbounded or delayed batching, changes to retry behavior, data lost on restart, an incompatible bulk contract, and combined filters returning a union instead of the documented intersection."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Early evidence is the weekly report's lack of a time filter, query.js using some rather than every, the absence of a bulk route, and tests that cover individual filters but not their combination. Controlled boundary cases and a 500-ID request can expose remaining contract and capacity problems before delivery."
    },
    {
      "id": "K12.1",
      "basis": "S2",
      "answer": "Terms requiring interpretation are until loop, back-chain, optional ralph-style loop, resolved-style heads-up, just got one, right away, batch, usual weekly-report window, filters together, idempotent close, bulk call and OldTracker."
    },
    {
      "id": "K12.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The supplied installed-skill descriptions settle until-loop, backchain and optional ralph-loop at a high level. Repository documentation and code establish resolved notifications, filter intersection, lifecycle, existing worker timing, audit and the weekly report's actual behavior. The repository does not establish the recent-notice interval, batching policy, a weekly time window, a bulk endpoint or OldTracker's role. External search was not used because this exercise permits only local read-only inspection; discovery should seek authorized internal evidence before relying on defaults."
    },
    {
      "id": "K12.3",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The usual window points to the weekly report and its operators, but scripts/weekly-report.js counts all listed tickets and operations documentation specifies a Monday schedule, not a date filter. The claimed existing idempotence and combined filters point to the transition, query and API documentation; those sources show a repeat close conflicts and filters currently combine with OR."
    },
    {
      "id": "K12.4",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Best provisional readings are a rolling seven-day closure window, a five-minute recent-resolution threshold, grouping by requester within each bulk call and delivery on the next enabled worker tick. Confidence is low because the request supplies no numbers or exact policy. OldTracker is treated only as context for the explicit retry-logic boundary, with no migration work inferred."
    },
    {
      "id": "K13.1",
      "basis": "S12",
      "answer": "Immediate delivery and batching compete for timing; idempotence and a 409 can coexist as unchanged effects on a repeat request despite a different response. The mandatory running-server exit applies to the eventual delivery run, while this intake explicitly prohibits starting a server. No other numerical contradiction is established."
    },
    {
      "id": "K13.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The report has no implemented weekly time window, despite the request's recollection; query.js applies OR despite the documentation's AND rule; there is no bulk-close route; repeated close already returns 409 without a second change; and notifications are worker-driven rather than literally immediate."
    },
    {
      "id": "K13.3",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Take idempotence to mean no repeated effects with 409 on repeat; honor documented AND filters; make close notices due promptly through the existing worker and batch within a bulk request; use provisional time-window and suppression defaults only after discovery searches for contrary evidence. Discovery should establish repository and authorized internal facts first; a product owner can confirm the unresolved business choices, but the unattended run records defaults rather than waiting."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "S1 [fact] Target the help desk service in this folder; this intake is read-only, implements nothing and starts no server, with no separate deployment granted. S2 [fact] Notify requesters on closure in the resolved-notification style and avoid a recent duplicate; [open, discovery then recorded default] establish what counts as a notice and its recency before fixing suppression, otherwise assume pending or sent within five minutes for the same ticket and requester, which blocks the final suppression contract. S3 [fact] Dispatch promptly and prevent bulk-close spam; [open, discovery then recorded default] establish grouping and delivery constraints before fixing the batch contract, otherwise group eligible closures by requester within one bulk call and make them due on the next enabled worker tick, which rules out a guarantee of literal immediate delivery and blocks final timing and message shape. S4 [fact] Make closed tickets selectable by the weekly-report window; [open, discovery then recorded default] inspect report code, operations documentation and authorized help desk sources for the intended window and closure timestamp first, otherwise assume a rolling seven-day UTC closure window, which blocks the final parameter and boundary contract. S5 [fact] Apply documented filters together as an intersection. S6 [fact] Verify the current behavior; source inspection already shows OR in matchesFilters, contrary to the documented AND, but no tests were run. S7 [fact] The later delivery run must back-chain first and continue until tests pass and a running server demonstrates the outcomes; this intake does neither implementation nor server execution. S8 [fact] Overnight Ralph-style work is optional and must be capped if used; [recorded default] do not invoke it, so a cap is not currently needed. S9 [fact] Require meaningful boundary tests and accurate, economical documentation. S10 [fact] Leave outbox retry logic unchanged and do not expand scope into OldTracker migration. S11 [fact] Add no third-party dependencies. S12 [fact] Preserve effect idempotence while returning 409 for an already closed ticket and recording the closer; [recorded default] use the existing caller-supplied X-Actor or system fallback, which does not authenticate an agent and rules out a verified-person guarantee. S13 [fact] Support 500 eligible tickets in one bulk call without a specified latency target; [open, discovery then recorded default] establish existing consumer expectations and body-size feasibility before fixing the new bulk API, otherwise use POST /tickets/bulk-close with an ids array and atomic rejection of invalid or ineligible requests, which blocks final mixed-result and error semantics. [Discovery source hints, not verified facts] The help desk team and support leads, repository README, API and operations documents, decision records, weekly-report script, tests, service modules and any authorized internal report documentation or owners are leads; no team channel, private repository or internal site has been identified. Store any sensitive locator only in an authorized private store, never KNOWLEDGE/. [Interaction and state] Actors are agents, requesters, operators and API consumers; the runtime identifies an actor only by an optional unverified header, shares process-owned ticket, audit and outbox state across callers, retains it across restarts only with DATA_FILE, and exposes internal endpoints without documented per-user hiding; requesters receive notification content through the configured output and relay. Existing single-process ownership is the recorded default and rules out cross-process coordination guarantees. [Visibility] The intended result becomes usable through the service HTTP API and notification output after implementation and verification; an isolated source checkout remains at its snapshot until final return, and any deployed target reflects the outcome only after release."
